@kind(writ)

#[ Writ: docs ]

Rules for `docs/` itself.

Scope is this directory. `docs/README.tmt` is the map -- what is where, and
where to start reading. This is the rulebook behind it: it says nothing
about what any document contains, only about where a document goes, what
language it is in, and which files are written by a tool rather than a
person.

Only the author edits this file. An agent that finds a rule in its way
proposes an edit here and stops.

##[ document-placement ]

A new document goes where it belongs by what it *is*, not by what it is
about.

@table[
[ The document is... ][ Put it in ]
[ normative -- it defines what the language accepts and what it means ][ `spec/` ]
[ instructional -- it shows how to get something done ][ `guide/` ]
[ a runnable sample document ][ `examples/` ]
[ a statement of what Tomet is and why it exists ][ `fact/` ]
[ an idea not yet decided ][ `design/ideas/` ]
[ a rule this repository must obey ][ the nearest `.writ.tmt` ]
]

Two of these carry an obligation. A document in `spec/` has to be kept in
step with `tomet-syntax-parser`. A document in `examples/` has to pass
`just docs-check`, because it is a real document and not a listing.

Two kinds of document have no home here on purpose.

A dated record of a decision has none: git is that record. A decision worth
carrying forward is written where it applies -- beside the rule it
justifies, or in the doc comment of the code it explains -- and the
alternatives that were rejected are written next to the one that was taken,
not in a separate file ordered by date.

Prose about the Rust workspace has none either. What a crate is and why it
exists belongs in that crate's `//!`, which dies with the crate and so
cannot outlive what it describes. How the layers depend on each other is
the root `.writ.tmt`'s `crate-layering`. Build and packaging commands are
the root `README.md`'s. `docs/develop/architecture.md` used to hold all
three at once; by the time it was removed, seven of its claims had gone
false -- three dependency lists, half the CLI's subcommands, and a
grammar-freeze process whose document did not exist.

---[ Why ]---

The split that earns its keep is `spec/` against `guide/`. Before the
restructure these were `ja/specifications/` and `ja/builtins/`, which split
spec from spec: a reader had to check both every time. Splitting on
normative against instructional means a reader picks one and is done.

The rejected alternative is filing by topic -- a directory per feature, each
holding its spec and its guide and its examples together. It reads well for
the person who already knows which feature they want, and badly for everyone
else, which is the wrong way round for documentation.

---[ Guard ]---

None yet. `twrit` can check the cheap half: every directory under `docs/`
appears in the table above, so a new directory nobody described is a
failure rather than a silence.

##[ docs-language ]

`spec/`, `guide/`, `examples/` and `fact/` are written in **Japanese**.
Japanese is the default, so filenames carry no `.ja` suffix and there is no
`ja/` directory level. English translations, if they are ever added, go
under `docs/en/` mirroring the same tree.

Every `.writ.tmt` is written in **English**.

`design/ideas/` is whichever language the thinking happened in. It is a
sketchpad, and forcing a translation step onto a sketch is how sketches stop
being written.

---[ Why ]---

The line is not about audience for its own sake -- it is about what each
document sits next to. A writ is read in the same pass as the code it
governs and cross-links with in-code doc comments, which are English by
`AGENTS.md`'s rule. `spec/` and `guide/` are read by people using Tomet,
and Japanese is what they are written for.

---[ Guard ]---

No kana in any `.writ.tmt`. Currently true.

```bash
find . -name '.writ.tmt' -not -path './target/*' \
  -exec grep -lP '[\x{3040}-\x{30ff}]' {} +
```

##[ generated-md-not-edited ]

`.md` under `spec/` and `guide/` is exported from the sibling `.tmt` via
`@config(export:)`. Do not hand-edit it. Edit the `.tmt` and re-export.

`.gitattributes` marks those two paths `linguist-generated` so GitHub
collapses them in diffs, scoped by path so that the hand-written `.md` under
`develop/` and `design/` stays a normal reviewable file.

This rule governs no file today: nothing under `spec/` or `guide/` declares
`@config(export:)`, and no `.md` exists there. It is written down because
the `.gitattributes` entries are already in place, and because the first
export will otherwise arrive with nobody having decided this.

`docs/README.tmt` is not exported either, for a different reason. `tomet
export` escapes backticks in prose (`` `spec/` `` becomes ``\`spec/\``) and
emits a `<div>` for the `@settings` header, so the rendered output would be
worse than the source. The consequence is that GitHub shows no rendered
index for `docs/`. Fix those two export bugs and this can become generated
like the rest.

---[ Guard ]---

```bash
tomet export --check .
```

Re-renders every source that declares an output and compares it against
what is on disk, writing nothing. A file that differs, or is missing
entirely, exits non-zero. Better than a rule asking people to remember not
to hand-edit: the failure names the file and the fix is to re-run the same
command without `--check`.

It governs one file today, `docs/exports/readme.tmt`. Nothing under `spec/`
or `guide/` declares an output yet.
